  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong on the core pressure: the draft allows exact-byte opaque store/forward and explicit unsupported-pcid failure, but it addresses the problem at the envelope dispatch layer rather than fully specifying unknown CID/codec handling.",
    "strengths": [
      "Explicit unknown-pcid policy allows opaque store/forward without speculative parsing.",
      "Very clear separation between envelope dispatch and payload interpretation.",
      "Mandatory signature slots leave room to preserve authenticity across mixed-version forwarding."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The scenario is about unknown CID/object codecs; this draft addresses unknown payload protocol handlers more directly than unknown CAS codecs.",
      "Advertisement and indexing behavior for unknown objects is not spelled out.",
      "Opaque payloads reduce semantic auditability until a handler exists."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Opaque forwarding needs external size, abuse, and retention controls.",
      "Unknown sig_pcid behavior is unspecified and could create interoperability splits."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Should unknown sig_pcid use the same explicit store/forward rule as unknown pcid?",
      "How should a peer advertise or fetch an unknown typed object when only the CID/codec is unavailable locally?",
      "What local promise-accounting record should Dave keep after storing but not interpreting the object?",
      "How are pcid handlers discovered and preserved over long durations without a central registry?"
    ],
